// glyph-a — a LETTERFORM STUDY, not an icon board. Nothing here ships on its own; the
// winner becomes the A inside sort-ascending / sort-descending and their alpha variants.
//
// The whole difficulty is the size. Two characters stack inside the mark with 1.5 of air
// between them, which leaves 8.25 · 6.25 · 5.25 of ink per character at 24 · 20 · 16 — at 16
// that is four stroke-widths tall. Every candidate here was drawn at 16 FIRST, at 5.25 of ink
// and 4.0 of centreline, and worked up; nothing was drawn at 24 and scaled.
//
// THE AXES, and how the 25 were spent:
//   apex treatment      5   A B C D E     drawn arc · sharp corner · sharp with a low bar ·
//                                         both strokes crossing past · one stroke crossing past
//   apex radius         4   F G H I       1.2x · 1.4x · 1.6x · 2x C10's minimum
//   splay / proportion  5   J K L M N     w/h 0.72 · 0.83 · 1.05 · 1.14 · 1.24
//   crossbar height     4   O P Q R       25% · 31% · 44% · 50% of the centreline height
//   crossbar width      3   S T U         inset · flush with the legs' outer edges · overhanging
//   combinations        4   V W X Y       wide + low bar · apex off-centre · the synthesis ·
//                                         the best a sharp apex can do
// NO CANDIDATE HAS A FLAT APEX. Every one comes to a point or to a drawn arc.
//
// WHAT WAS MEASURED THIS SESSION — every counter by flood fill on the drawing itself, at 96
// samples per unit, in a rasteriser written for this study (there is no rasteriser binary on
// this machine). The counter is reported as the bounding box of the enclosed white, so it is
// the honest air, not a mixed reading.
//
// 1. THE DRAWN ARC IS WORTH SEVEN TIMES THE COUNTER OF A SHARP CORNER. Same letter, same
//    width, same bar: A's counter is 0.9x1.24 at 16 (area 0.56), B's is 0.38x0.4 (area 0.08).
//    A round linejoin rounds only the OUTSIDE of the corner; a drawn arc rounds the inside as
//    well, and the inside of the apex is where the counter starts.
// 2. A BIGGER APEX ARC DOES NOT SHUT THE COUNTER — it opens it. This board was briefed the
//    other way ("the inner counter starts at ink top + 2r, so doubling the radius leaves 0.25
//    of counter"), and the drawings do not reproduce it. Held to a fixed ink height, the
//    counter's top sits at ink top + ONE STROKE whatever the radius is, because the arc's
//    outer edge is r + stroke/2 from its centre and its inner edge is r - stroke/2: the
//    difference is the stroke, and r cancels. Measured at 16, radius 0.625 -> 0.875 -> 1.0 ->
//    1.25: counter 0.9x1.24 -> 1.13x1.25 -> 1.27x1.25 -> 1.54x1.25. What a bigger arc really
//    costs is the LETTER: the legs flatten from 19.7 to 12.2 degrees and by 2x the apex reads
//    as a horseshoe (I). The answer is two grid steps above C10's minimum, not the minimum
//    itself and not double it.
// 3. THE COUNTER'S SHAPE IS FIXED BY THE SPLAY, ITS SIZE BY THE BAR. The counter is a taper,
//    so its width over its height is exactly 2·tan(leg angle) on every candidate here, and no
//    crossbar height changes that — the bar only decides how much taper you get. So width is
//    the only control on counter PROPORTION and bar height the only control on counter AREA.
// 4. AT 5.25 OF INK, 1.5 OF COUNTER IN BOTH DIRECTIONS COSTS EITHER WIDTH OR APEX RADIUS.
//    Only X reaches it (1.48x1.5) and it buys it with a 1.4x apex arc and w/h 1.05. The
//    narrowest drawings, J and K at w/h 0.72 and 0.83, close to 0.46 and 0.63 and read as a
//    dark mass at real pixels. The honest answer to "can this character be drawn well at 5.25
//    of ink": yes, but not at type proportions — it has to be about as wide as it is tall.
//
// TWO THINGS MARUTI SENT BACK, both fixed here on all 25 at all three masters:
//
// A. THE CROSSBAR MUST NOT SHOW PAST THE LEGS' OUTER EDGES ("should look smooth like this").
//    It did, by up to 0.07 at 24, and the cause was snapping the bar's ends OUTWARD to the
//    grid. The geometry: a round cap of radius half the stroke lies entirely inside a stroke
//    of the same width exactly when its centre is on, or inside, that stroke's centreline —
//    so the longest bar with an unbroken outer line down each leg is the one whose ends land
//    ON the leg centrelines. They are now measured on the path that actually ships and
//    rounded INWARD at the last decimal place (A at 24 goes from 9.75 to 9.77). Checked by
//    walking the ink outward from each leg centreline at 128 samples per unit: 0 proud on 24
//    of the 25, at every master. The exception is U, the overhanging-bar variant, which shows
//    0.82 · 0.56 · 0.54 past on purpose — it is on the board so the reading can be rejected.
//    S, the inset bar, is a different question and keeps its own deliberate shortfall.
//
// B. THE FILLED A IS NO LONGER A THICKENED WIRE ("is there a way the bold version doesn't
//    look like a blob?"). Every candidate but the two crossed ones is now a real SOLID
//    letterform: its outer edge is the outline drawing's own outer edge point for point (the
//    same leg edges, the same apex arc at radius r + half a stroke, the same round feet, ink
//    box equal to within 0.004), and its counter is CUT AS A HOLE. That is the whole
//    difference: a thickened wire loses its counter from both sides at once, a cut counter
//    keeps whatever air it is given however heavy the letter gets. Weight is added only where
//    the counter never pays for it — the bar is deepened downward by 0.5 · 0.25 · 0.375 and
//    the legs are pulled in below it by as much as leaves 1.5 of air in the notch between
//    them, which for the narrow candidates (J, K) is nothing at all. Verified by rasterising
//    the fill and the stroked drawing and comparing them pixel by pixel.
//    The two crossed apexes (D, E) cannot carry it: cut as one solid the crossing welds shut
//    and the variant disappears, so they keep the thickened skeleton and do go dark — which
//    is itself evidence against a crossed apex at this size.
//
// A SECOND COUNTER MEASURE came out of B, from the repo's own facingGap (what lint §5f
// reads): the counter's NECK, the narrowest span that faces itself square-on. It ranks the
// board independently of the flood-fill box, and it ranks it the same way. X is the only
// candidate clear of §6's 1.5 at both 24 and 20, and has the widest neck at 16 (1.39);
// A measures 1.02 and 0.85. Every solid candidate whose neck is under 1.5 warns in lint, and
// that warning is this study's question restated, not a defect — the outline twin has exactly
// the same counter and escapes measurement only because §5b's turn gate never fires on a
// two-legged path.
//
// Rasterised at exactly 16x16 and looked at, every candidate, beside shipped list-ordered.

